WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.660
Selfish elves, a Santa kidnapping, and&nbsp;
a candy-fueled creeper. We love "Elf,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.680
but there are a few things you won't be&nbsp;
able to ignore next time you watch it.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:15.480
At its heart, "Elf" is a classic&nbsp;
fish-out-of-water comedy, but with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:20.160
the added twist being that the fish — Buddy&nbsp;
the Elf — doesn't fit in back in the water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.840
either. The movie's opening scenes&nbsp;
show us some great physical comedy as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:29.880
the six-foot-three Will Ferrell squeezes&nbsp;
himself into elf-sized showers and beds.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:35.400
But here's the thing. Why don't the elves&nbsp;
just, you know, make him a bed? One of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.600
things that makes the movie feel heartwarming&nbsp;
and Christmasy is that the other elves up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.700
at Santa's workshop are generally pretty nice&nbsp;
to Buddy. They even take time to reassure him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.420
that he's got his own special talents when&nbsp;
he starts to feel like he doesn't fit in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:52.860
and even if they get frustrated behind his&nbsp;
back, it's clear that they care about him.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:58.140
So why, in the 30 years that he's been there,&nbsp;
haven't they made the slightest adjustment to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.920
their housing situation so that he's&nbsp;
not destroying his spine every night?

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.780
It's not like it would take them that long.&nbsp;
Building things is literally all the elves do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:12.120
and a king-sized bed frame and mattress surely&nbsp;
wouldn't take as much time to cobble together as,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:17.640
say, building a PlayStation 5 from scratch. He's&nbsp;
not even the only one there who needs human-sized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.860
furniture, since Santa Claus also lives there&nbsp;
and has been doing so for about 1500 years!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:28.380
Even if the elves aren't the ones who made his&nbsp;
furniture, just order another mattress and put it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:33.390
on the floor. It's not great, but it's better than&nbsp;
sleeping with all your limbs hanging off the bed.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:37.620
The odd thing about Buddy the Elf&nbsp;
is that he never seems to notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:42.660
that he's roughly three times taller than&nbsp;
everyone he knows. Well, almost everyone.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:48.480
The only exception is Santa Claus. Buddy's still&nbsp;
taller than Santa, but not to the extent that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.220
is with the other elves. If anything, Buddy&nbsp;
should probably assume that he's a Santa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.420
or at least some other kind of human.&nbsp;
The only reason that Buddy thinks he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.740
an elf at all is that after accidentally&nbsp;
kidnapping him when Buddy was an infant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:07.680
Santa pulls the very un-Santa-like move of&nbsp;
keeping him and handing him off to the elves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.340
This, by the way, is not what you&nbsp;
should do if you ever find a baby.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:16.560
Buddy's naivete is at the core of the film,&nbsp;
but there's a fine line between his earnest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:22.020
good-natured belief that a random diner really&nbsp;
did perfect the world's best cup of coffee and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:26.277
the kind of oblivious ignorance of just not&nbsp;
being able to put simple clues together.

00:02:26.277 --> 00:02:33.951
"You did it! Congratulations! World's&nbsp;
Best Cup of Coffee! Great job everybody!"

00:02:34.148 --> 00:02:37.320
You'd think that if you can&nbsp;
figure out how to build 85&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:41.635
Etch-a-Sketches in a single day, you'd be a&nbsp;
little bit better at putting things together.

00:02:41.792 --> 00:02:50.400
"Past the sea of swirly, twirly gumdrops. And&nbsp;
then I walked through the Lincoln Tunnel."

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.320
This is going to sound pretty nitpicky for a&nbsp;
movie where we're willing to accept that Santa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:59.340
Claus can fly around the world in a single&nbsp;
night giving all the good children presents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:04.440
but the fact that Buddy walks from the North&nbsp;
Pole to Manhattan is a little ridiculous.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:10.200
There are a lot of reasons for that, primarily&nbsp;
the fact that it's a straight shot of about 3,400&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:15.540
miles from Santa's workshop to Manhattan. Even&nbsp;
if we're willing to accept that the iceberg he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:21.240
floats off on takes him down to Canada, that still&nbsp;
leaves him in Nunavut with 2,900 miles left to go.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:25.380
We'll give you that his elf clothes are&nbsp;
magical and able to keep him warm at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:30.840
North Pole and below, but still. No matter how&nbsp;
hyped up he is on maple syrup and candy canes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.200
that's a long and potentially&nbsp;
— or, let's be real here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.940
almost certainly — fatal journey for&nbsp;
someone who's completely unprepared for it.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:44.760
Now here's why this isn't as much of a nitpick&nbsp;
as it seems like. The scene sticks out because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:50.280
this is a movie with Santa in it. The guy has a&nbsp;
magic flying sleigh that can cover 212,000,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.920
miles in a single night! Sure, he's a busy guy&nbsp;
around the holidays, but are we really meant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.900
believe that Santa Claus can't take 0.4 seconds&nbsp;
out of his day so that he doesn't have to deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:05.869
with a singing penguin finding a dead body in&nbsp;
the Candy Cane Forest after the spring thaw?

00:04:06.311 --> 00:04:08.160
"You like sugar, huh?"

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:09.900
"Is there sugar in syrup?"

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:10.740
"Yes."

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:12.189
"Then, yes!"

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:16.860
One of the biggest sources of comedy&nbsp;
throughout "Elf" is Buddy's ridiculously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:21.120
candy-centric diet. After all, he&nbsp;
hails from a land of Christmas cheer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:25.140
that was inspired by the Rankin-Bass&nbsp;
stop-motion specials of the '60s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:30.807
so he grew up on a steady diet of candy canes and&nbsp;
describes the North Pole's four food groups as,

00:04:30.807 --> 00:04:35.007
"Candy, candy canes, candy corns, and syrup."

00:04:35.007 --> 00:04:40.620
Of course, that means Buddy should be straight-up&nbsp;
dead. You probably figured this out for yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:45.420
in the days after Halloween when you were&nbsp;
a kid, but a diet that consists entirely of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:49.920
candy isn't just unhealthy, it's also&nbsp;
a great way to get Type 2 Diabetes.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:55.140
Sure, you could explain it away by saying that&nbsp;
all the candy at the North Pole is suffused with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:59.520
Christmas magic that makes it delicious and&nbsp;
nutritious, but after he comes to New York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.260
Buddy keeps up his standard habits, drowning&nbsp;
a plate of spaghetti with an entire bottle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:09.720
maple syrup and washing it down by slamming an&nbsp;
entire two-liter bottle of Coca-Cola. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:15.300
the few things we see him eat in the movie that&nbsp;
aren't candy are a bottle of booze and a jar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:20.220
full of cotton balls. Fun fact: in real life,&nbsp;
those cotton balls were actually cotton candy.

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:25.980
If you need any evidence that wolfing down that&nbsp;
much sugar was a bad idea, look no further than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:31.080
Will Ferrell himself. In interviews about the film&nbsp;
since its release, he's talked about how he lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:36.420
sleep while the movie was being filmed thanks&nbsp;
to the constant sugar intake. Multiply that by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:41.580
30 plus years worth of sugar-coated meals, and&nbsp;
you've got health problems that go way beyond a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:46.620
little insomnia and into the territory of having&nbsp;
your heart explode while chugging maple syrup.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.720
"Did you sleep okay last night?"

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.232
"Great! I got a full 40 minutes!"

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.340
Just five years away from "Iron Man,"&nbsp;
Jon Favreau was the man who directed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:00.300
"Elf," and he even has a small part as&nbsp;
the Hobbs family's friendly physician.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:03.720
He also provides the voice of the&nbsp;
North Pole's resident narwhal.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:06.641
"Bye Buddy. Hope you find your dad."

00:06:06.641 --> 00:06:11.400
And while we're talking about Favreau's&nbsp;
doctor, we'd be remiss if we didn't cover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:16.440
what might be the worst medical decision&nbsp;
ever committed to film. After performing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:21.360
DNA test to confirm that, yes, Buddy&nbsp;
is in fact Walter's biological son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:27.300
the doctor advises Walter to take Buddy home&nbsp;
to meet his wife and son in hopes that he'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.680
eventually come to terms with his situation&nbsp;
and "drop the whole elf thing and move on."

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:35.940
Obviously, that all works out for Buddy&nbsp;
and the Hobbs family, but by any measure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:40.800
this is some truly terrible advice. Both&nbsp;
Walter and the doctor are operating under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:45.240
the assumption that Buddy is extremely&nbsp;
delusional. Any medical professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:49.320
worth his stethoscope would most likely suggest&nbsp;
that they get Buddy some immediate treatment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:54.060
psychiatric or otherwise, before his&nbsp;
disconnect with reality leads to more violence.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:07:00.480
That's right. More violence. The scene with&nbsp;
the blood test happens directly after Walter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:05.880
bails Buddy out of jail after he assaults a&nbsp;
department store Santa at Gimbels. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:11.160
the doctor is confronted with a delusional&nbsp;
man with a very recent history of violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:16.140
and his solution to this problem is "take him home&nbsp;
to your wife and kid." Here's hoping the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:20.866
thing the Hobbs family does after the credits&nbsp;
roll is get themselves a new general practitioner.

00:07:20.866 --> 00:07:26.460
"Your father…well…he's on the naughty list."

00:07:26.722 --> 00:07:28.336
"Noooo!"

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:33.780
The driving conflict of "Elf" is that Walter&nbsp;
Hobbs, Buddy's long-lost human father, has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:39.360
landed himself squarely on the naughty list. The&nbsp;
reason? Well, in addition to just being a fairly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:44.580
unpleasant person — and all those murders he did&nbsp;
in "The Godfather" — Walter has been neglecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:49.260
his family and focusing too much on his work.&nbsp;
This is the plot to plenty of Christmas movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:53.940
about learning how to love your family more&nbsp;
effectively under the bootheel of consumerism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:59.220
but there's something different about Walter's&nbsp;
story. He's genuinely terrible at his job.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:03.960
Normally, the person in this kind of plot&nbsp;
who's obsessing over their work is at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:07.800
successful in that arena, which makes it more&nbsp;
effective when they have to choose what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:12.240
really important. Walter's got plenty of money&nbsp;
— that apartment at Central Park West doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:17.280
come cheap — but as far as the work goes, he's&nbsp;
hardly a success. His company's floundering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:20.580
and their downturn is a direct&nbsp;
result of decisions that he made.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:26.520
Or rather, due to decisions that he didn't make.&nbsp;
Despite filling the role of the workaholic who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:31.620
spends too much time at the office, Walter doesn't&nbsp;
actually seem to care about anything that he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:36.120
doing at work, to the point of approving a&nbsp;
proof of a book that was missing two pages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:41.700
Even the most inept employee would notice if&nbsp;
he was initialing two gigantic blank pages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:46.560
so the simple fact is that Walter just doesn't&nbsp;
care. That raises a pretty grim question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:51.360
though. If he's neglecting his family for&nbsp;
work, but he's not actually doing any work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:55.620
is he just sitting there, taking as much&nbsp;
time away from his wife and son as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.620 --> 00:09:00.240
can so that he can stare off into space&nbsp;
and contemplate oblivion? That is dark.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:05.306
"I like it. I like it."

00:09:05.306 --> 00:09:10.620
The major drama at the climax of the film centers&nbsp;
around whether Santa's sleigh is going to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:15.360
to fly without the engine that's been keeping it&nbsp;
going all these years. See, without the engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:20.340
the sleigh has to rely entirely on the magic of&nbsp;
Christmas cheer to power it around the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:24.900
and according to the Clausometer, there's just&nbsp;
not enough Christmas spirit to do the job.

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:30.840
But that doesn't make sense. This is a world&nbsp;
where Santa Claus is 100 percent, actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:35.460
factually real, with a toy factory at the&nbsp;
North Pole staffed by cheerful singing elves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:39.900
a talking snowman, and everything that goes&nbsp;
along with him. He brings presents to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:43.680
good children all over the world, which&nbsp;
means that there's no disillusionment or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:47.100
disappointment from kids who didn't get&nbsp;
what they wanted on Christmas morning.

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:51.120
We also know from the movie that&nbsp;
getting a present does in fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:55.020
increase your Christmas spirit,&nbsp;
as Michael, Walter's other son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:59.640
boosts the Clausometer a little bit when he&nbsp;
gets the skateboard he's always wanted. So if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:05.040
Santa and the elves have been doing their job&nbsp;
— and as far as we know, they have — then there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:08.880
should be more than enough Christmas spirit&nbsp;
to go around just from that, let alone all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:13.149
the general feel-good sentiments of peace on&nbsp;
Earth, goodwill, hot chocolate, and so on.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.300
One of the things that makes "Elf" such a&nbsp;
favorite around the holidays is that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:23.580
an almost relentlessly cheerful film without&nbsp;
getting too sugary sweet — Buddy's spaghetti&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:28.500
notwithstanding. In fact, the closest it&nbsp;
gets to anything you'd call "dark" would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:33.480
be the depiction of the Santa-hating Central&nbsp;
Park Rangers at the end. Oh, and that one part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:37.560
where Buddy commits felony assault on an innocent&nbsp;
man for the crime of dressing up as Santa Claus.

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:40.800
But even if Buddy's sudden temper&nbsp;
is slightly out of character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:45.240
that all makes perfect sense in the context&nbsp;
of the movie. What happens after, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:49.860
seems like a stretch. Since the original&nbsp;
Gimbels Santa is no longer available,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:54.780
the toy department's manager has to&nbsp;
fill in, and frankly, that's ridiculous.

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:58.800
If you've ever been to Macy's around&nbsp;
Christmastime, then you already know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:02.940
that these stores take their Santa setup&nbsp;
very seriously. It's a lot more complicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:06.300
than just one dude sitting on a big chair,&nbsp;
asking kids what they want for Christmas.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:10.680
The Gimbels we see in the movie might&nbsp;
not be as dedicated to it as Macy's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:17.160
of course. Historically, the real-life Gimbels&nbsp;
was Macy's competitor until shutting down in 1987,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:21.900
with their rivalry cementing itself into&nbsp;
Christmas movie history as a plot point in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:27.720
the original "Miracle on 34th Street." Still,&nbsp;
the idea that a store that big wouldn't have at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:32.400
least a couple of backup Santas is somehow more&nbsp;
difficult to believe than the flying reindeer.

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:37.500
Buddy's romance with Jovie, a young&nbsp;
woman working as an elf at Gimbels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:42.180
played in a star-making turn by Zooey&nbsp;
Deschanel, ends up being pretty sweet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:47.100
but it has a rocky start. Most people don't&nbsp;
wind up going on dates with strangers who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:51.120
sneak up on them in the shower, no matter how&nbsp;
nice they are about your singing afterwards.

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:56.400
Considering that "Elf" has a runtime of just over&nbsp;
90 minutes, it makes sense that things have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:01.200
move pretty fast to get everything wrapped up&nbsp;
by the time the credits roll. Still, Jovie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:06.120
awfully quick to forgive Buddy for creeping up&nbsp;
on her in the shower, especially since the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:10.800
two interactions she has with him involve him&nbsp;
beating up the guy playing Santa Claus and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:15.180
telling her that his tongue swells up around&nbsp;
her and asking if she wants to "go eat food."

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:18.480
"I feel really warm when I… am around you."

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:22.980
The fact that she goes out with him at&nbsp;
all, let alone ends up in happy elfin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:27.360
matrimony after it's all said and done,&nbsp;
is, well ... it's a Christmas miracle.

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:31.680
The weirdest part of all of this might be&nbsp;
one that Jovie would have no way of knowing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:37.140
but that we in the audience can peg as&nbsp;
what might be the only lie Buddy tells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:41.580
in the whole movie. When she asks if he was&nbsp;
spying on her in the shower, he replies,

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:43.560
"I didn't know you were naked."

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:49.560
This seems plausible, given Buddy's naive nature.&nbsp;
The thing is, he definitely knew she was in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:54.960
shower, and we actually see Buddy showering in&nbsp;
an elf-sized tub earlier in the movie. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:59.160
he did have his hat on at the time, so&nbsp;
maybe elves are a rare class of never-nudes.

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:02.580
"Oh I'm sorry, I need you&nbsp;
to be completely undressed."

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:05.000
"I am completely undressed."

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:10.440
There are any number of ways to tell a child that&nbsp;
they are adopted, though perhaps no worse way than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:15.060
for them to overhear it from two co-workers&nbsp;
badmouthing their Etch-a-Sketch production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:19.980
output. Buddy learns the hard way that he is not&nbsp;
an elf, and while you might reasonably expect him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:25.440
to come to that conclusion after a decade or two,&nbsp;
it's also not unreasonable to expect Papa Elf and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:30.600
Santa to have had that conversation directly,&nbsp;
saving Buddy a bundle of unnecessary trauma.

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:35.400
Santa's judgment in regards to the whole&nbsp;
Buddy situation becomes even more suspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:39.780
considering the fact that he's apparently&nbsp;
had all the information needed to reunite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:44.460
Buddy with his father for years. It would be&nbsp;
one thing if Buddy were simply an anonymous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:48.720
orphan who crawled into Santa's sack&nbsp;
one year; in that case, it would make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:52.620
certain amount of sense for Santa to take the&nbsp;
human baby in and raise it at the North Pole.

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:58.260
But Santa also knows the name of Buddy's mother,&nbsp;
Susan Wells, the circumstances of her death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.260 --> 00:14:03.660
the name of his still-living father, Walter Moss,&nbsp;
Walter's job and the location of his office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:08.520
and the fact that Walter is on the Naughty List.&nbsp;
He only reveals this information when his hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:13.440
is forced, as Buddy inadvertently discovers the&nbsp;
truth. While it's hard to hate on Santa Claus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:18.593
all of this points to a serious lapse&nbsp;
of judgment on the Big Guy's part.

00:14:18.593 --> 00:14:19.860
The scene featuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:24.900
future "Game of Thrones" star Peter Dinklage&nbsp;
as hotshot children's book author Miles Finch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:30.720
is cringe-inducing, but purposely so. Walter&nbsp;
has bribed Miles to come into the office and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:34.800
help his clueless in-house writers with&nbsp;
a new book pitch. He's hoping this pitch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:38.580
will be the thing that gets Walter back&nbsp;
in good with his boss and save his job.

00:14:38.580 --> 00:14:44.100
But before Miles can get into his potential&nbsp;
storyline, Buddy barges into the meeting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:49.680
mistakes the man for an elf. Miles takes offense&nbsp;
at Buddy's sincere excitement to see a fellow elf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:54.180
as a crack about his height, and while he&nbsp;
tries to keep his cool, eventually Buddy's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:59.580
"insults" become too much to bear; Miles runs&nbsp;
across the boardroom table and drop-kicks him.

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:05.580
That all makes sense, even if — in illustrating&nbsp;
Buddy's insensitivity — the film itself gets a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:11.340
little insensitive. What doesn't make much sense&nbsp;
is how no one else in the scene — not Walter, his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.340 --> 00:15:16.740
writers, or his secretary — does anything to break&nbsp;
up the fight, as Miles chokes Buddy to the ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:21.480
and breaks every lighting fixture and piece of&nbsp;
glassware in the room along the way. They all just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:27.060
sit there, apparently frozen in shock, until the&nbsp;
scene ends with Walter angrily disowning Buddy.

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:32.520
Every year, it seems that someone on the&nbsp;
internet discovers and is newly horrified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:38.160
by the scene in which Buddy, banished to the&nbsp;
Greenway Press mailroom for irritating Walter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:42.420
makes friends with — and accidentally&nbsp;
gets day drunk with — a worker played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:47.100
by Mark Acheson. As they recline on a pair&nbsp;
of rolling carts full of padded envelopes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:52.200
declaring themselves best friends, Mailroom&nbsp;
Guy, as he is credited, laments the direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:57.000
that his life has taken; he just goes with the&nbsp;
flow, but now he needs to get out of the flow.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:59.340
"I'm 26 years old, I got nothing to show for it."

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:01.320
"You're young. You're so young."

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:07.260
Acheson was in fact 46 years old when the&nbsp;
film was released, and while he may appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:12.720
relatively youthful for a 46-year-old, he&nbsp;
makes for an alarmingly craggy, gravel-voiced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:18.000
26-year-old. Mailroom Guy works in a chaotic&nbsp;
processing room with no source of natural light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:22.620
he drinks from a half-pint of hard liquor&nbsp;
on the job, and only has the job thanks to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.620 --> 00:16:27.780
prison work-release program. So, the impact of the&nbsp;
gag makes for a worthy suspension of disbelief.

